# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

Manages a rate limit rule for the Netskope AI Gateway (AIG). Rate limit rules control how many requests per time unit are allowed through a given appliance, with optional filtering by token group, AI provider, model, or MCP server capability.

## `apply_on` Controls Which Criteria Fields Are Valid

The `criteria.apply_on` field determines which traffic type the rule applies to, and which other `criteria` fields are valid:

| `apply_on` | Valid criteria fields | Invalid (will be stripped) |
|---|---|---|
| `"ai"` | `ai_provider_ids`, `models`, `token_group_ids` | `mcp_server_ids`, `prompts`, `resources`, `tools` |
| `"mcp"` | `mcp_server_ids`, `prompts`, `resources`, `tools`, `token_group_ids` | `ai_provider_ids`, `models` |

The provider automatically strips criteria fields that do not apply to the selected `apply_on` value. You can safely include them in configuration and they will be ignored at the API level.

## Rate Limit Scope

The limit (`requests` per `unit`) applies **per appliance**. If `appliance_ids` is empty, the rule applies to all appliances. If specified, it applies only to the listed appliances (max 5).

## Finding AI Provider and Appliance IDs

`ai_provider_ids` and `appliance_ids` are UUIDs. Use the [`netskope_aig_ai_provider_list`](../data-sources/aig_ai_provider_list) data source to find AI provider IDs, and the [`netskope_aig_appliance_list`](../data-sources/aig_appliance_list) data source for appliance IDs:

```hcl
data "netskope_aig_ai_provider_list" "all" {}

locals {
  provider_id = {
    for p in data.netskope_aig_ai_provider_list.all.ai_providers : p.name => p.id
  }
}

resource "netskope_aig_rate_limit" "example" {
  # ...
  criteria = {
    apply_on       = "ai"
    ai_provider_ids = [local.provider_id["openai"]]
  }
}
```

## Token Groups

`token_group_ids` filters the rule to traffic authenticated with tokens from the listed groups. An empty list means the rule matches all token groups.

## Custom Response Message

Use `response` to return a human-readable error message when the rate limit is exceeded. Clients receive this in the API response body.

